  "Almost everyone has had the experience of sitting down on a Sunday evening to finish a piece of homework that was supposed to take an hour, only to find themselves still working at midnight. What is strange is not that we sometimes misjudge how long a task will take, but that we keep making the same mistake in the same direction. {u1|We rarely finish things much earlier than planned, yet we regularly finish them much later, even when we have done similar tasks many times before.} Psychologists have a name for this habit: the planning fallacy*.",
  "The planning fallacy is not limited to students. Most of us have seen this in our own lives. A school festival that is planned for weeks still needs a final night of panic, and a room that someone planned to paint in a single afternoon is still half done when the weekend ends. Even the people who plan such things for a living make the same error. Building projects, new computer systems and public events are often completed months or years behind schedule and well over budget. What makes the pattern so puzzling is that the people involved are usually experienced and well informed. If asked how long similar projects have taken in the past, they can often give an accurate answer. When they estimate their own project, however, they seem to forget that knowledge completely.",
  "One explanation is that {u2|we imagine the future as a smooth story in which everything goes according to plan.} When we picture writing an essay, we see ourselves opening the books, making notes and typing steadily until the end. We do not picture the friend who calls in the middle, the website that will not load, or the paragraph that has to be rewritten three times. Each of these interruptions may seem unlikely on its own, but the chance that at least one of them will happen is very high. Because our imagined story leaves them all out, our estimate is almost certain to be too short.",
  "A second explanation concerns the way we think about our past. When a task took longer than expected, we tend to blame special circumstances: the printer happened to break, or a relative happened to visit. Since we treat each delay as an exception, we do not learn from it, and the next plan is just as hopeful as the last. Our wishes may also play a part. If we want to believe that we can finish the job before the weekend, it becomes easier to ignore the evidence that we probably cannot.",
  "Fortunately, there are ways to reduce the problem. {u3|The most effective one is surprisingly simple: instead of examining every step of the task one by one, look at how long similar tasks have taken in the past.} If your last three reports each took about six hours, it is wise to plan for six hours this time too, however confident you feel. Another useful step is to divide a large task into smaller parts and estimate each part separately, which makes it harder to overlook steps that seemed too small to mention. Some people also ask a friend to estimate for them, since an outsider is less influenced by hopes about how things will go.",
